{
  "component": "Checkbox Group",
  "description": "A container that lays out five Checkbox Field instances in a single arrangement, switchable between horizontal and vertical. It carries no label, legend or validation of its own, so those must come from the surrounding form layout. PURPOSE: Groups related checkboxes so a user can pick any number of options from a set. The group itself handles arrangement and spacing only; each option's checkbox, label and description live inside the nested Checkbox Field. BEHAVIOUR: The orientation property switches the stacking direction. Horizontal places fields in a row, vertical stacks them in a column. Both variants use Space/300 between fields. Selection state, focus and disabling are handled per field, not at group level, so the group has no states of its own. COMPOSITION: Five instances of Checkbox Field, each containing a Checkbox and Label frame and a Description Row frame. A Check instance supplies the tick mark inside the box. AI code generators should look for existing Checkbox Field and Check implementations in the development codebase before building new ones. USAGE: Use when several checkbox options belong to one question and multiple answers are allowed. Use a Radio Group instead when only one answer is allowed, and a Switch when the control toggles a setting immediately. Keep the horizontal variant for two or three short labels; longer labels or descriptions read better vertically. CODE GENERATION NOTES: Render as a fieldset with a legend, or an element with role=\"group\" and an accessible name, and let the child fields own their input elements. The number of options is fixed at five in Figma, so treat the children as a repeatable slot and map them from data. Shared name, change handlers, required state and error messaging belong on the group in code even though they are not modelled in the design. Flex direction should follow the orientation prop, and the horizontal variant should be allowed to wrap on narrow viewports.",
